  "imageName": "arrow.right.to.line",
  "intro": "You learned basic accusative in A1 for direct objects. Now we add the crucial distinction: ANIMATE vs. INANIMATE. For masculine nouns, this matters! Animate masculine nouns (people, animals) take genitive-like endings in accusative: Я вижу студента (not студент). Inanimate masculine and all neuter nouns stay the same as nominative. Feminine rules remain unchanged.",
  "rules": [
    "Accusative marks the direct object - what receives the action: Я читаю книгу.",
    "The key question: is the noun alive (animate) or not? This determines masculine accusative.",
    "Feminine nouns always change in accusative: -а → -у, -я → -ю.",
    "Neuter nouns NEVER change in accusative.",
    "Personal names follow the same rules: вижу Ивана (animate), вижу Москву (feminine)."
  ],
  "examples": [
    "Я вижу студента. (I see the student.), animate masculine",
    "Я вижу стол. (I see the table.), inanimate masculine",
    "Она любит брата. (She loves her brother.), animate masculine",
    "Он читает книгу. (He reads a book.), feminine",
    "Мы видим окно. (We see the window.), neuter"
  ],
